CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe criteria for promotion appointments of firefighting officials and other necessary matters. <Amended on Mar. 10, 2020>

Article 2 (Scope of application) Except as otherwise provided in statutes or regulations, promotion appointments of firefighting officials shall be in accordance with this Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 27, 2010]

Article 3 (Classification of promotion appointments) Promotion appointments of firefighting officials shall be classified into promotion appointments by review, promotion appointments by test, and special promotion appointments.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 27, 2010]

Article 4 (Ratios by type of promotion appointment and determination of the number of persons to be promoted) (1) The number of persons to be promoted among firefighting officials shall be determined by the appointing authority (including a person delegated with the authority to make appointments under Article 3 of the Decree on the Appointment of Firefighting Officials; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply), taking into account actual and anticipated vacancies for the relevant year. <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; May 24, 2004; Dec. 27, 2010; Nov. 5, 2019; Mar. 10, 2020; Aug. 13, 2024>
  (2) If promotion appointments by review and promotion appointments by test are conducted concurrently under the proviso of Article 14(2) of the Firefighting Officials Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Act"), 60 percent of the number of persons to be promoted shall be allocated to promotion appointments by review, and 40 percent to promotion appointments by test. <Amended on Apr. 26, 1986; Sep. 9, 1999; Mar. 31, 2005; Dec. 27, 2010; Mar. 10, 2020; Jan. 2, 2024>
  (3) In determining the number of persons to be promoted by rank under paragraph (2), if the number of persons for special promotion appointments is determined separately under paragraph (4), the number of persons to be promoted for the relevant rank shall be the number obtained by subtracting the number of persons for special promotion appointments from the initial number of persons to be promoted. <Added on Sep. 9, 1999>
  (4) If the number of persons to be promoted to the rank of fire captain or lower is determined under paragraph (1), the number of persons to be promoted by special promotion may be separately determined within 30 percent of those to be promoted to the relevant rank; provided, in cases of special promotion under Article 38(1)1, 4, and 5 and Article 38(2), such ratio may be exceeded. <Amended on Jan. 2, 2024>

Article 5 (Minimum length of service required for promotion) (1) To be promoted, a firefighting official shall have served in the relevant rank for at least the period under each of the following classifications: <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010; Dec. 28, 2012; Mar. 10, 2020; Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. A fire chief: 3 years;
  2. A deputy fire chief: 2 years;
  3. A fire captain: 2 years;
  4. A fire lieutenant: 1 year;
  5. A fire sergeant: 1 year;
  6. A senior firefighter: 1 year;
  7. A firefighter: 1 year.
  (2) Periods of leave of absence, release from position, disciplinary action, and restriction on promotion appointment under Article 6(1)2 shall not be included in the period under paragraph (1); provided, the following periods shall be included in the period under paragraph (1): <Amended on Dec. 28, 2012; May 6, 2015; May 2, 2017; Sep. 18, 2018; Dec. 24, 2018; Mar. 10, 2020; Aug. 31, 2021; Jan. 2, 2024; Jan. 24, 2025>
  1. The following periods during a leave of absence under Article 71 of the State Public Officials Act:
  a. Where a public official takes a leave of absence under Article 71(1)1 of the State Public Officials Act due to a disease or injury in the line of duty under the Public Officials' Accident Compensation Act, the period of such leave of absence;
  b. Where a public official takes a leave of absence under Article 71(1)3, 5, or 6 or Article 71(2)1 of the State Public Officials Act, the period of such leave of absence;
  c. Where a public official takes a leave of absence under Article 71(2)2 of the State Public Officials Act, a period equivalent to 50 percent of the period of such leave of absence;
  d. The period of leave of absence under Article 71(2)4 of the State Public Officials Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as "childcare leave"); provided, the period included under paragraph (1) shall not exceed 3 years per child when aggregated with the period during which a firefighting official serves as a firefighting official converted to part-time service in lieu of childcare leave under paragraph (8)3;
  2. The period of release from position where any of the following applies:
  a. Where a disciplinary action that constituted the grounds for a disposition of release from position imposed on a person under Article 73-3(1)3 of the State Public Officials Act is confirmed to be null and void or canceled by a decision of the appeals review committee or a court judgment (including cases where the competent disciplinary committee resolves not to impose disciplinary action upon a request for disciplinary action);
  b. Where a criminal case that constituted the grounds for a disposition of release from position imposed on a person under Article 73-3(1)4 of the State Public Officials Act is confirmed as not guilty by a court judgment;
  c. Where misconduct (hereafter in this Article referred to as "misconduct") that constituted the grounds for a disposition of release from position imposed on a person under Article 73-3(1)6 of the State Public Officials Act falls under both 1) and 2):
  1) Where, in relation to disciplinary procedures relating to such misconduct, any of the following applies;
  2) Where, as a result of an investigation or criminal investigation into such misconduct, any of the following applies.
  (3) If a retired firefighting official is appointed to a rank not higher than the rank he or she held at the time of retirement, the period during which he or she served, prior to retirement, in a rank not lower than the rank at the time of reappointment shall be included in the period under paragraph (1), limited to the rank at the time of reappointment. <Amended on Aug. 31, 2021>
  (4) If a person who had served under the status of a public official under other statutes or regulations is appointed as a firefighting official of at least fire sergeant, the period during which he or she served under his or her previous status shall be included in the period under paragraph (1), as converted in accordance with standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, limited to service within 10 years from the date of reappointment; provided, if a person is appointed as a firefighting official and is subsequently promoted, any period during which he or she served as another public official in a position corresponding to the rank to which he or she was promoted or a higher rank shall not be included in the period under paragraph (1). <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; Dec. 31, 2008; Dec. 27, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; May 6, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017; Mar. 10, 2020; Aug. 31, 2021>
  (5) The period during which a person served as a trainee of the Judicial Research and Training Institute under Article 72 of the Court Organization Act shall be included in the minimum length of service required for promotion of firefighting officials of deputy fire chief or lower under paragraph (1). <Added on Mar. 31, 2009; May 6, 2015; Mar. 10, 2020>
  (6) If a firefighting official is demoted or reduced in rank, the period during which he or she served in the rank to which he or she is demoted or reduced or in a higher rank shall be included in his or her length of service in the rank to which he or she is demoted or reduced. <Added on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (7) If a firefighting official who has been demoted or reduced in rank is promoted to his or her original rank, the period during which he or she served in the rank held prior to such demotion or reduction shall be included in his or her length of service in the original rank. <Added on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (8) The period of service of a firefighting official who works fewer hours than the normal working hours under Article 26-2 of the State Public Officials Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as a "firefighting official converted to part-time service") shall be included in the period under paragraph (1) in accordance with the following criteria: <Added on Dec. 27, 2010; Dec. 24, 2018; Mar. 10, 2020; Jan. 24, 2025>
  1. In the relevant rank, if a firefighting official serves as a firefighting official converted to part-time service for a period not exceeding 1 year, the entire period;
  2. In the relevant rank, if a firefighting official serves as a firefighting official converted to part-time service for a period exceeding 1 year, a period proportionate to his or her working hours;
  3. In the relevant rank, if a firefighting official is designated to serve as a firefighting official converted to part-time service in lieu of childcare leave, the entire period within 3 years per child.

Article 6 (Restriction on promotion appointment) (1) A firefighting official who falls under any of the following shall not be eligible for promotion appointment: <Amended on Mar. 31, 2009; Dec. 27, 2010; May 6, 2015; May 2, 2017; Jul. 17, 2018; Sep. 18, 2018; Dec. 11, 2018; Nov. 5, 2019; Mar. 10, 2020; Aug. 31, 2021; Mar. 28, 2023>
  1. A person who is subject to a request for disciplinary action or a request for disciplinary resolution, is under disciplinary action, is under release from position, is on leave of absence (excluding cases where a person on leave of absence due to a disease or injury in the line of duty under the Public Officials' Accident Compensation Act is specially promoted under Article 38(1)4 or 5), or is in a probationary appointment period;
  2. A person for whom the following period has not elapsed from the date on which the execution of a disciplinary action is completed [in cases of a disciplinary action due to any of the grounds under Article 78-2(1) of the State Public Officials Act or due to passive administration, drunk driving (including cases of refusal to take a sobriety test), sexual violence, sexual harassment, or commercial sex acts, the period obtained by adding 6 months, respectively]:
  a. Demotion or suspension: 18 months;
  b. Reduction of salary: 12 months;
  c. Reprimand: 6 months;
  3. Where a firefighting official is appointed from among public officials subject to other statutes or regulations with respect to disciplinary actions, a person who, under his or her previous status, has been subject to a disciplinary action of demotion and for whom 18 months have not elapsed from the date of completion of such disciplinary action, or who has been subject to disciplinary restriction, disciplinary training, or other similar disciplinary actions and for whom 6 months have not elapsed from the date of completion of such disciplinary action;
  4. A person who fails to complete the initial training course under Article 5 and subparagraph 1a of Appendix 1 to the Regulations on the Education and Training of Firefighting Officials;
  5. A person who fails to complete the management competency training course under Article 5 and subparagraph 1b of Appendix 1 to the Regulations on the Education and Training of Firefighting Officials;
  6. A person who fails to complete the firefighting policy manager training course under Article 5 and subparagraph 1c of Appendix 1 to the Regulations on the Education and Training of Firefighting Officials.
  (2) If a person who is within the period of restriction on promotion appointment under paragraph (1) is again subject to disciplinary action, the period of restriction on promotion appointment shall be calculated from the date on which the restriction period for the previous disciplinary action ends; and if a person who is within the period of restriction on promotion appointment due to disciplinary action takes leave of absence or is subject to a disposition of release from position, the remaining period of restriction on promotion appointment due to such disciplinary action shall be calculated from the date of reinstatement. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010; May 6, 2015; Aug. 31, 2021>
  (3) If a firefighting official, after being subject to disciplinary action, receives decorations or medals, an award for exemplary public officials, a commendation from the Prime Minister or higher, or an award for the adoption and implementation of a proposal in the relevant rank, the period of restriction on promotion appointment under paragraph (1)2 and 3 may be reduced by 1/2. <Amended on May 6, 2015>
  (4) Deleted. <May 6, 2015>
  (5) Deleted. <Jan. 5, 2007>

Article 6-2 (Promotion based on length of service) (1) The period for promotion based on length of service under Article 15 of the Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as "promotion based on length of service") shall be calculated in accordance with the method for calculating the minimum length of service required for promotion under Article 5(2) through (8). <Amended on Mar. 10, 2020>
  (2) If firefighting officials falling under any of the following are appointed by promotion based on length of service pursuant to the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 15(1) of the Act, the period specified in each subparagraph may be reduced from the period for promotion based on length of service: <Added on Aug. 31, 2021>
  1. A firefighting official who is in a personnel exchange period or has experience in personnel exchange under Article 48(1)1 of the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials: A period equivalent to 1/2 of the personnel exchange period;
  2. A firefighting official whose performance in the implementation of major tasks, such as national policy tasks, is outstanding or whose attitude toward proactive administration is exemplary: 1 year.
  (3) The number of firefighting officials for whom the period for promotion based on length of service is reduced under paragraph (2)2 may be limited by the Minister of Personnel Management. <Added on Aug. 31, 2021>
  (4) Candidates for promotion based on length of service shall be persons listed in the promotion eligibility list under Article 11. <Amended on Jul. 17, 2018; Aug. 31, 2021; Jun. 27, 2024>
  (5) When conducting a promotion review for appointment to the rank of fire captain based on length of service, the appointing authority shall not make promotion appointments based on length of service in excess of the number corresponding to 50/100 of those eligible for promotion based on length of service in the relevant agency, calculated on a yearly cumulative basis (where there is a fraction, 1 person shall be added). <Amended on Jun. 27, 2024>
  (6) If the appointing authority intends to conduct a promotion review under paragraph (5), he or she shall report the persons eligible for promotion based on length of service and the number of persons to be promoted based on length of service in the relevant agency to the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency no later than 20 days before the date of promotion based on length of service. <Added on Mar. 28, 2023>
  (7) If the appointing authority deems it necessary for the efficient operation of personnel administration, he or she may prepare promotion eligibility lists separately according to periods of service as fire lieutenant. <Amended on Mar. 10, 2020; Aug. 31, 2021; Mar. 28, 2023>
  (8) If the requirements for promotion based on length of service are satisfied, a promotion review may be conducted from 5 days before the date on which the period for promotion based on length of service is completed. <Amended on Aug. 31, 2021; Mar. 28, 2023>
  (9) Matters necessary for the method of promotion based on length of service and personnel administration, other than those provided in paragraphs (1) through (8), shall be determined by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Aug. 31, 2021; Mar. 28, 2023>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 21, 2011]

CHAPTER II PERFORMANCE, CAREER, AND EDUCATION AND TRAINING RATINGS

Article 7 (Performance ratings) (1) Performance ratings shall be conducted for firefighting officials of the rank of fire chief or lower, and the results thereof shall be reflected in various personnel management matters, including promotion, transfer, special advancement in salary step, payment of performance bonuses, education and training, and position management. <Amended on Jan. 20, 2003; Mar. 10, 2020>
  (2) Performance ratings shall assess the performance, job performance capability, attitude toward duties, potential for development, etc. of the relevant firefighting official. <Amended on Jan. 20, 2003>
  (3) Performance ratings shall be conducted so that the distribution of rating results conforms to the ratios specified in the following subparagraphs for each rank of the persons subject to rating; provided, if the number of persons subject to rating is too small to make the application of such ratios reasonable, or if there are no persons falling under subparagraph 4a, the appointing authority need not apply such ratios; in such cases, the ratio specified in subparagraph 4 shall be added to that of subparagraph 3: <Amended April 26, 1986, April 16, 1991, March 10, 2020>
  1. Excellent: 20 percent;
  2. Very good: 40 percent;
  3. Good: 30 percent;
  4. Fair: 10 percent.
  (4) The results of performance ratings shall not be disclosed; provided, the head of a fire service agency under subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Decree on the Appointment of Firefighting Officials may, upon completion of performance ratings, notify the firefighting official subject to rating of the results thereof. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2019; Aug. 13, 2024>
  (5) Matters necessary for the standards, timing, methods, etc. of performance ratings shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 8 (Exceptions to performance ratings) (1) If a firefighting official has served for less than 1 month during the period subject to performance ratings due to leave of absence, release from position, or other reasons, performance ratings shall not be conducted. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (2) If a firefighting official has served for less than 1 month due to education and training, such as overseas secondment, the average of the results of the 2 most recent performance ratings shall be deemed to be the performance rating of the relevant firefighting official until the first regular performance rating is conducted after he or she returns to duty. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (3) If a firefighting official serves on secondment to a State agency or a local government for at least 6 months, performance ratings shall be conducted taking into account the opinion of the agency to which he or she is seconded. <Added on Jan. 20, 2003>
  (4) If a firefighting official is transferred, the performance rating sheet of the relevant firefighting official shall be transferred to the agency to which he or she is transferred; provided, if a performance rating is conducted within 1 month after such transfer to an agency having a different rating authority, the former agency shall conduct a performance rating corresponding to the period of service before the transfer and transmit it, and the receiving agency shall conduct a performance rating taking into account the results thereof. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (5) For a firefighting official who is newly appointed or promoted after a regular performance rating, a performance rating shall be conducted on the first regular performance rating date after 2 months have elapsed; provided, if a firefighting official who has been demoted is promoted, a performance rating shall be conducted immediately based on the performance rating in the rank held before the demotion. <Amended on Jan. 5, 2007>
  (6) If a firefighting official is exchanged between the National Fire Agency and the Special Metropolitan City, a Metropolitan City, a Special Self-Governing City, a Do, or a Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as "City/Do"), or between Cities/Dos, the performance rating received before such personnel exchange shall be deemed to be the performance rating of the relevant firefighting official. <Amended on Mar. 10, 2020>

Article 9 (Career ratings) (1) Career ratings of firefighting officials shall assess the length of service in the relevant rank and shall be reflected in the preparation of the promotion eligibility list.
  (2) Career ratings shall apply to firefighting officials of the rank of fire chief or lower who have completed the minimum length of service required for promotion under Article 5. <Amended on Apr. 16, 1991; Mar. 10, 2020>
  (3) Career ratings under paragraph (1) shall be conducted based on the personnel records of the relevant firefighting official, and, if deemed necessary, the accuracy of such records may be inquired into and verified.
  (4) Career shall be classified into basic service and additional service, and the basic service and additional service by rank shall be as follows: <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010; Jul. 17, 2018; Mar. 10, 2020; Aug. 31, 2021; Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. Basic service:
  a. Fire chief, deputy fire chief, and fire captain: 3 years from the rating reference date;
  b. Fire lieutenant and fire sergeant: 2 years from the rating reference date;
  c. Senior firefighter and firefighter: 1 year and 6 months from the rating reference date;
  2. Additional service:
  a. Fire chief: 2 years preceding the basic service period;
  b. Deputy fire chief: 4 years preceding the basic service period;
  c. Fire captain and fire lieutenant: 3 years preceding the basic service period;
  d. Fire sergeant: 1 year preceding the basic service period;
  e. Senior firefighter and firefighter: 6 months preceding the basic service period.
  (5) Matters necessary for the timing, methods, and calculation of periods for career ratings, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 10 (Education and training ratings) (1) Education and training ratings of firefighting officials shall be conducted with respect to firefighting officials of the rank of fire chief or lower, and the education and training subject to rating by rank shall be as follows:
  1. Fire chief: Ratings of completion of the Firefighting Policy Administrator Training ratings under subparagraph 1c of Appendix 1 to the Regulations on the Education and Training of Firefighting Officials (hereinafter referred to as "Firefighting Policy Administrator Training ratings");
  2. Deputy fire chief, fire captain, and fire lieutenant: Education and training ratings falling under any of the following items:
  a. Ratings of completion of the Management Competency Training under subparagraph 1b of Appendix 1 to the Regulations on the Education and Training of Firefighting Officials (hereinafter referred to as "Management Competency Training ratings");
  b. Ratings of specialized education and training under subparagraph 2 of Appendix 1 to the Regulations on the Education and Training of Firefighting Officials, limited to education and training ratings prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety (hereinafter referred to as "Specialized Education and Training ratings");
  c. Ratings of on-the-job training under subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Regulations on the Education and Training of Firefighting Officials (hereinafter referred to as "On-the-Job Training ratings");
  d. Ratings of physical fitness assessment prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety (hereinafter referred to as "Physical Fitness Assessment ratings");
  3. Fire sergeant or lower: Specialized Education and Training ratings, On-the-Job Training ratings, Physical Fitness Assessment ratings, and ratings relating to professional competency prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety (hereinafter referred to as "Professional Competency ratings").
  (2) The scores for education and training ratings under paragraph (1) shall be as follows:
  1. Fire chief: Firefighting Policy Administrator Training ratings, 10 points;
  2. Deputy fire chief, fire captain, and fire lieutenant: Management Competency Training ratings, 3 points; Specialized Education and Training ratings, 3 points; On-the-Job Training ratings, 4 points; and Physical Fitness Assessment ratings, 5 points;
  3. Fire sergeant or lower: Specialized Education and Training ratings, 3 points; On-the- Job Training ratings, 4 points; Physical Fitness Assessment ratings, 5 points; and Professional Competency ratings, 3 points.
  (3) No ratings shall be assigned for education and training of a person who fails to meet the completion requirements or graduation requirements under Article 17 of the Regulations on the Education and Training of Firefighting Officials.
  (4) If a person who is to be appointed on probation or who has been appointed on probation under Article 10 of the Act graduates from the initial training course under subparagraph 1a of Appendix 1 to the Regulations on the Education and Training of Firefighting Officials, such training shall be deemed Specialized Education and Training ratings received in the rank to which he or she is to be appointed, and ratings shall be assigned accordingly.
  (5) Matters necessary for the timing, methods, etc. of education and training ratings shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 28, 2023]

CHAPTER III PROMOTION ELIGIBILITY LIST

Article 11 (Preparation of promotion eligibility list) (1) A promotion eligibility list shall be prepared by rank, for fire chiefs who meet the requirements for promotion, in accordance with the ratios of 70 percent for performance rating scores, 20 percent for career rating scores, and 10 percent for education and training rating scores, and for firefighting officials of the rank of deputy fire chief or lower, in accordance with the ratios of 70 percent for performance rating scores, 15 percent for career rating scores, and 15 percent for education and training rating scores; in such cases, if any of the following applies, additional points shall be added as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety: <Amended on Apr. 26, 1986; Dec. 31, 1991; Sep. 9, 1999; Jan. 20, 2003; Jan. 5, 2007; Dec. 31, 2008; Dec. 27, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jan. 6, 2015; May 6, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017; Mar. 10, 2020; Aug. 31, 2021; Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. If he or she holds a certificate;
  2. If he or she has obtained a bachelor's, master's, or doctoral degree or has excellent language proficiency;
  3. If he or she has experience working in hardship or undesirable departments;
  4. If he or she has outstanding job performance;
  5. If he or she has experience in personnel exchanges implemented by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency for the balanced development of firefighting administration.
  (2) The promotion eligibility list under paragraph (1) shall be prepared by the following persons: <Amended on Dec. 31, 1991; Aug. 25, 1994; Sep. 9, 1999; Jan. 20, 2003; May 24, 2004; Jan. 28, 2011; Sep. 17, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jan. 6, 2015; May 6, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017; Mar. 10, 2020; Oct. 14, 2021; Mar. 28, 2023; Aug. 13, 2024>
  1. Firefighting officials belonging to the National Fire Agency; firefighting officials of the rank of fire captain or higher belonging to the National Fire Service Academy or the National 119 Rescue Headquarters; firefighting officials of the rank of deputy fire chief or higher belonging to the National Fire Research Institute; and the heads of local fire service academies who are fire chiefs: Commissioner of the National Fire Agency;
  2. Firefighting officials of the rank of fire lieutenant or lower belonging to the National Fire Service Academy or the National 119 Rescue Headquarters, or firefighting officials of the rank of fire captain or lower belonging to the National Fire Research Institute: the head of the National Fire Service Academy, the head of the National 119 Rescue Headquarters, or the head of the National Fire Research Institute;
  3. Firefighting officials for whom appointment authority is exercised by the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as the "Mayor/Do Governor") under Article 3(1) and (5)1 and 3 of the Decree on the Appointment of Firefighting Officials (excluding cases falling under subparagraph 4): The Mayor/Do Governor;
  4. Firefighting officials of the rank of fire lieutenant or lower belonging to a local fire service academy, the Seoul Comprehensive Disaster Prevention Center, a fire station, a 119 Special Rescue Unit, or a fire experience center: The head of a local fire service academy, the head of the Seoul Comprehensive Disaster Prevention Center, the head of a fire station, the head of a 119 Special Rescue Unit, or the head of a fire experience center.
  (3) If the person authorized to prepare the promotion eligibility list under paragraph (2) differs from the head of the institution in which the competent promotion review committee under Article 19 is established, the head of the institution in which the competent promotion review committee is established shall integrate the promotion eligibility lists prepared by the person authorized to prepare under paragraph (2) and shall prepare an integrated promotion eligibility list in order of priority. <Added on Apr. 26, 1986; Jan. 6, 2015>
  (4) The promotion eligibility list and the integrated promotion eligibility list shall be prepared as of April 1 and October 1 each year. <Amended on Apr. 26, 1986; Jan. 20, 2003; Jan. 5, 2007; May 6, 2015; Mar. 28, 2023>

Article 12 (Priority among persons with the same score) (1) If the total rating scores on the promotion eligibility list are the same, priority shall be determined in the following order: <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>
  1. A person with a higher performance rating score;
  2. A person who has served longer in the relevant rank;
  3. A person who has served longer in the rank immediately below the relevant rank;
  4. A person who has served longer as a firefighting official.
  (2) If priority cannot be determined even under paragraph (1), the person authorized to prepare the promotion eligibility list shall determine the order of priority.

Article 13 (Adjustment of promotion eligibility list) (1) The person who prepares the promotion eligibility list shall adjust the promotion eligibility list if, after preparing the promotion eligibility list, any of the following applies: <Amended on Jan. 20, 2003; Dec. 27, 2010; Mar. 10, 2020; Mar. 28, 2023>
  1. Where any person is transferred out or transferred in;
  2. Where any person retires;
  3. Where any person reaches the minimum length of service required for promotion;
  4. Where any person becomes subject to or ceases to be subject to restrictions on promotion appointments under Article 6;
  5. Where any person undergoes a performance rating after the regular rating date under Article 8(5);
  6. Where any person becomes subject to or ceases to be subject to exclusion from promotion review under Article 23;
  7. Where any person undergoes a rerating due to the discovery of facts inconsistent with the rating after a career rating or an education and training rating has been conducted;
  8. Where any person is promoted or is confirmed as a promotion candidate;
  9. Where any person is transferred to an institution with a different unit for preparing the promotion eligibility list.
  (2) The promotion eligibility list under paragraph (1) may be adjusted until the day preceding the date on which a promotion review or a promotion test is conducted. <Amended on Apr. 16, 1991; Aug. 25, 1994; Mar. 28, 2023>
  (3) Deleted. <Mar. 28, 2023>

Article 14 (Validity of promotion eligibility list) The promotion eligibility list shall take effect from the day following the preparation reference date; provided, if the promotion eligibility list is adjusted or deleted under Article 13, it shall take effect from the date of such adjustment.

Article 15 (Submission of promotion eligibility list) (1) The head of the institution preparing the promotion eligibility list shall submit the promotion eligibility list to the head of the institution that conducts a promotion review for the relevant rank within 30 days after the preparation reference date for the promotion eligibility list.
  (2) If the promotion eligibility list is adjusted or deleted under Article 13, it shall be submitted without delay, accompanied by documents proving the grounds therefor.

CHAPTER IV PROMOTION REVIEW

Article 16 (Promotion review) The promotion review of firefighting officials shall be conducted at least once a year on a date determined by the head of the institution in which a promotion review committee is established. <Amended on Jan. 5, 2007; Aug. 31, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 26, 1986]

Article 17 (Composition of the Central Promotion Review Committee) (1) The Central Promotion Review Committee under the main clause of Article 16(1) of the Act shall be composed of at least 5 and not more than 7 members, including 1 Chairperson. <Amended on Mar. 10, 2020>
  (2) Deleted. <Dec. 27, 2010>
  (3) Members under paragraph (1) shall be appointed or commissioned by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency from among firefighting officials of a higher rank than those subject to promotion review or external experts. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Mar. 10, 2020; Jul. 7, 2025>
  (4) Notwithstanding paragraph (3), members of the Central Promotion Review Committee established to review whether to grant special promotion appointments under Article 38(1)5 or (2), whether to revoke special promotion appointments under Article 41-2(3)3, and whether to grant ex post approval of special promotion appointments under the latter part of Article 42(3) shall be the following persons; in such cases, members under subparagraph 2 shall constitute a majority: <Added on Jul. 7, 2025>
  1. A person designated by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency from among firefighting officials of a higher rank than those subject to special promotion review (including public officials corresponding to a higher rank and members of the Senior Executive Service; hereafter in this subparagraph the same shall apply); provided, if there are insufficient firefighting officials of a higher rank than those subject to special promotion review, a person designated by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency from among firefighting officials of the same rank may be included;
  2. A person commissioned by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency who falls under any of the following items:
  a. A person who has served for at least 10 years as a judge, a prosecutor, or an attorney-at-law;
  b. A person who has served for at least 10 years as an assistant professor or higher at a university;
  c. A person who has expertise in personnel administration, labor affairs, or firefighting, or who has served in a relevant field for at least 10 years.
  (5) The Chairperson under paragraph (1) shall be designated by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency from among the members, and the Chairperson under paragraph (4) shall be commissioned by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency from among the members under paragraph (4)2. <Added on Jul. 7, 2025>
  (6) No member shall concurrently serve as a member of promotion review committees for 2 or more ranks during the relevant period for promotion review; provided, this shall not apply if there are insufficient persons eligible to serve as members or in cases of special promotion review. <Amended on Jul. 7, 2025>
  (7) The Chairperson shall represent the promotion review committee, exercise general supervision over its affairs, and if the Chairperson is unable to perform his or her duties due to an unavoidable reason, a member designated in advance by the Chairperson shall act on his or her behalf. <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; Jan. 5, 2021; Jul. 7, 2025>
  (8) A member under paragraph (4) shall be excluded from the review by the promotion review committee if he or she falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Added on Jul. 7, 2025>
  1. Where a member is or was related by kinship to a person subject to review of the relevant agenda item (referring to a relative under Article 777 of the Civil Act);
  2. Where a member has been involved in facts constituting the requirements for special promotion appointment or for revocation thereof with respect to a person subject to review of the relevant agenda item; provided, this shall not apply if a public official has been involved in such facts in the course of performing his or her duties.
  (9) A member under paragraph (4) shall recuse himself or herself from review of the relevant agenda item if he or she falls under any of the grounds for exclusion under the subparagraphs of paragraph (8) or if he or she determines that there exist circumstances under which it is difficult to expect fairness in review. <Added on Jul. 7, 2025>

Article 18 (Composition of general promotion review committee) (1) "Affiliated agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 16(1) of the Act refers to the National Fire Service Academy, the National 119 Rescue Headquarters, and the National Fire Research Institute. <Amended on Jan. 6, 2015; Mar. 10, 2020>
  (2) A general promotion review committee under Article 16(1) of the Act shall be comprised of not fewer than 5 and not more than 9 members, including 1 Chairperson. <Amended on Mar. 10, 2020>
  (3) The Chairperson and members of a general promotion review committee shall be appointed or commissioned by the head of the institution in which the relevant general promotion review committee is established from among the following persons: <Amended on Apr. 16, 1991; Dec. 31, 1991; Aug. 25, 1994; Oct. 19, 1995; Sep. 9, 1999; May 24, 2004; Mar. 31, 2005; Jun. 12, 2006; Dec. 27, 2010; Nov. 19, 2014; Jan. 6, 2015; May 6, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017; Mar. 10, 2020>
  1. For a general promotion review committee of the National Fire Agency, firefighting officials of a higher rank than those subject to promotion review or external experts;
  2. For a general promotion review committee of a City/Do, firefighting officials of a higher rank than those subject to promotion review or external experts;
  3. For a general promotion review committee of the National Fire Service Academy, the National 119 Rescue Headquarters, or the National Fire Research Institute, firefighting officials of a higher rank than those subject to promotion review;
  4. Deleted. <May 6, 2015>
  (4) A member of a general promotion review committee shall not concurrently serve as a member of promotion review committees for 2 or more ranks during the relevant promotion review period; provided, this shall not apply if there are insufficient persons eligible to serve as members or in cases of special promotion review or promotion based on length of service. <Added on Dec. 28, 2012>
  (5) Article 17(7) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the operation of a general promotion review committee. <Amended on Dec. 28, 2012; Jul. 7, 2025>
  (6) Notwithstanding paragraphs (2) and (3), Article 17(1), (4), (5), (8), and (9) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the composition of a general promotion review committee of a City/Do established to review whether to grant a special promotion appointment under Article 38(1)5 or (2), whether to revoke a special promotion appointment under Article 41-2(3)3, and whether to grant ex post approval of a special promotion appointment under the latter part of Article 42(3). In such cases, "Central Promotion Review Committee" shall be construed as "general promotion review committee," and "Commissioner of the National Fire Agency" shall be construed as "Mayor/Do Governor". <Added on Jul. 7, 2025>

Article 19 (Jurisdiction of promotion review committees) The jurisdiction of promotion review committees shall be as follows: <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017; Jul. 17, 2018; Mar. 10, 2020; Aug. 13, 2024>
  1. Central Promotion Review Committee of the National Fire Agency: Promotion review for firefighting officials of the National Fire Agency and its affiliated agencies and the head of a local fire service academy at the rank of fire chief for promotion to chief superintendent;
  2. General promotion review committee of the National Fire Agency: Promotion review for firefighting officials of the National Fire Agency and its affiliated agencies for promotion to the rank of fire chief or lower (excluding cases under the jurisdiction of promotion review committees under subparagraphs 4 and 5);
  3. General promotion review committee of a City/Do: Promotion review for firefighting officials over whom the Mayor/Do Governor exercises appointment authority under Article 3(1) and (5)1 and 3 of the Decree on the Appointment of Firefighting Officials;
  4. General promotion review committee of the National Fire Service Academy or the National 119 Rescue Headquarters: Promotion review for firefighting officials affiliated therewith for promotion to the rank of fire captain or lower;
  5. General promotion review committee of the National Fire Research Institute: Promotion review for firefighting officials affiliated therewith for promotion to the rank of deputy fire chief or lower.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jan. 6, 2015]

Article 20 (Meetings of promotion review committees) (1) A promotion review committee shall be convened if the head of the institution in which the promotion review committee is established deems it necessary.
  (2) A quorum for meetings of the promotion review committee shall be at least 2/3 of the members, and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present; provided, a promotion review committee formed under Article 17(4) (including where Article 17(4) applies mutatis mutandis under Article 18(6)) shall adopt resolutions by the concurring vote of a majority of all members. <Amended on Jul. 7, 2025>
  (3) Meetings of a promotion review committee shall not be open to the public.

Article 21 (Executive secretary of promotion review committees) (1) A promotion review committee shall have 1 executive secretary and 1 or more clerks.
  (2) The executive secretary and clerks shall be appointed by the head of the institution in which the relevant promotion review committee is established from among public officials in charge of personnel affairs in the relevant institution.
  (3) The executive secretary shall, under the direction of the Chairperson, handle the affairs of the promotion review committee, and the clerks shall assist the executive secretary.

Article 22 (Persons subject to promotion review) Promotion review shall be conducted for the number of persons corresponding to the classifications in the Appendix, in order from those ranked higher on the promotion eligibility list under Article 11(1) or the integrated promotion eligibility list under paragraph (3) of that Article.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Nov. 23, 2021]

Article 23 (Exclusion from promotion review) A promotion review committee shall exclude from promotion review any of the following persons subject to promotion review: <Amended on Jan. 5, 2007>
  1. Deleted; <Dec. 27, 2010>
  2. A person who falls under any subparagraph of Article 6(1);
  3. A person who is not eligible to take a promotion test under Article 36(1).

Article 24 (Criteria for promotion review) (1) A promotion review committee shall, in order to evaluate the ability of a person subject to promotion review to perform duties in the rank to which he or she is to be promoted, review the following matters: <Amended on Aug. 25, 1994; Jan. 20, 2003; Dec. 27, 2010>
  1. Performance: Performance ratings, career ratings, education and training ratings, etc. in the current rank;
  2. Positions held: Departments in which he or she has served and duties he or she has performed in the current rank, etc.;
  3. Ability to perform duties and character: Ability to perform duties, potential for development, attitude toward the State, integrity, etc.
  (2) Matters necessary for the criteria for evaluating the matters under paragraph (1) and other matters necessary for review procedures shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 25 (Reporting of results of promotion review) (1) When promotion review is completed, a promotion review committee shall, without delay, prepare the following documents and report them, in the case of the Central Promotion Review Committee, to the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency, and in the case of a general promotion review committee, to the head of the institution in which the committee is established: <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; May 24, 2004; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. A resolution on promotion review;
  2. A comprehensive evaluation report on promotion review;
  3. A list of persons selected for promotion appointment and those not selected.
  (2) The list of persons selected for promotion appointment under paragraph (1)3 shall be prepared in descending order of comprehensive evaluation scores from promotion review. <Amended on Apr. 26, 1986>

Article 26 (Preparation of candidate list for promotion by review) (1) An appointing authority or an appointment-recommending authority shall prepare a candidate list for promotion by review for persons selected for promotion appointment by a promotion review committee, in accordance with the order in the list of persons selected for promotion appointment under Article 25(2). <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (2) If a person listed in the candidate list for promotion by review is subject to a disciplinary action of a reduction of salary or heavier before promotion appointment, the appointing authority or the appointment-recommending authority shall remove such person from the candidate list for promotion by review.

Article 27 (Promotion appointment of promotion candidates) (1) If there are candidates for promotion by review and candidates for promotion by test, 60 percent of the number of promotion appointments shall be allocated to candidates for promotion by review and 40 percent to candidates for promotion by test. <Amended on Jan. 2, 2024>
  (2) In making promotion appointments of firefighting officials, appointments shall be made in accordance with the ranking in the candidate list for promotion by review and the candidate list for promotion by test; where persons holding the same ranking in each candidate list are appointed at different times, candidates for promotion by review shall be appointed first, followed by candidates for promotion by test; provided, candidates for special promotion may be appointed in preference to candidates for promotion by review and candidates for promotion by test. <Amended on Apr. 26, 1986; Jan. 2, 2024>
  (3) Promotion appointments by review shall be made in accordance with the ranking in the candidate list for promotion by review under Article 26. <Amended on Apr. 26, 1986>

CHAPTER V PROMOTION TEST

Article 28 (Principle of conducting tests) Promotion tests for firefighting officials (hereinafter referred to as "tests") shall be conducted by rank.

Article 29 (Delegation of authority to conduct tests) (1) Under the proviso of Article 11 of the Act, the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall delegate to a Mayor/Do Governor authority to conduct tests for promotion to ranks of fire sergeant or below for firefighting officials belonging to a City/Do. <Amended on Nov. 23, 2021>
  (2) If a Mayor/Do Governor conducts tests under paragraph (1), he or she may request the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency to prepare test questions. In such cases, matters necessary for the sharing of expenses for the preparation of test questions, etc. shall be determined through consultation between the Mayor/Do Governor and the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency. <Added on Mar. 10, 2020; Nov. 23, 2021>
  (3) Deleted. <Jan. 5, 2007>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 26, 1986]
  [Title Amended on Mar. 10, 2020]

Article 30 (Eligibility to take tests) Any person who meets the requirements in any of the following subparagraphs may take a test for the relevant rank: <Amended on Aug. 25, 1994; Mar. 31, 2005; Jan. 5, 2007; Dec. 27, 2010; Mar. 10, 2020>
  1. He or she shall have completed, as at the date of the first test, the minimum length of service required for promotion under Article 5(1);
  2. Deleted; <Dec. 27, 2010>
  3. He or she shall not be subject to restrictions on promotion appointment under Article 6(1);
  4. Deleted. <Sep. 9, 1999>

Article 30-2 Deleted. <Sep. 9, 1999>

Article 31 (Administration and public announcement of tests) (1) Tests shall be administered on a date determined by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency or a person to whom the authority to administer tests has been delegated under Article 29(1) (hereinafter referred to as the "authority administering the test"). <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; May 24, 2004; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Mar. 10, 2020>
  (2) If tests are to be administered, the date and time, place, and other matters necessary for the administration of tests shall be publicly announced not later than 20 days before the date of such tests.

Article 32 (Methods and procedures for tests) (1) Tests shall be administered separately as the first test and the second test; provided, the authority administering the test need not administer the second test if he or she deems it unnecessary. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (2) In principle, the first test shall be a multiple-choice written test; provided, it may include short-answer questions for each subject. <Amended on Jan. 5, 2021>
  (3) Deleted. <Dec. 27, 2010>
  (4) The second test shall be an interview test and shall assess the ability to apply knowledge required for the performance of duties and suitability. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (5) A person who does not pass the first test shall not be permitted to take the second test. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010>

Article 33 (Subjects of written tests) The subjects of written tests shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; Jan. 20, 2003; Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [Title Amended on Jan. 20, 2003]

Article 34 (Determination of passing of tests) (1) A person who obtains at least 40 percent of the full score in each subject and at least 60 percent of the full score in all subjects shall pass the first test. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (2) Deleted. <Dec. 27, 2010>
  (3) Persons who obtain at least 60 percent of the full score shall pass the second test, taking into account rewards and disciplinary actions, education and training ratings, and the ability to perform duties in the rank to which they are to be promoted, etc. <Amended on Dec. 27, 2010>
  (4) Final successful candidates shall be determined in descending order based on the total score calculated by combining 50 percent of the score in the first test, 10 percent of the score in the second test, and 40 percent of the total rating score on the most recently prepared promotion candidate list for the relevant rank; provided, if the second test is not administered, the score in the first test shall be weighted at 60 percent. <Amended on Apr. 16, 1991; Dec. 27, 2010>
  (5) If, in determining final successful candidates under paragraph (4), there are persons tied in score in excess of the number of persons to be appointed through promotion by test, final successful candidates shall be determined in descending order of their ranking on the promotion eligibility list. <Amended on Jan. 2, 2024>

Article 35 (Appointment of examiners) (1) The authority administering the test may appoint or commission as examiners any of the following persons in order to have them perform duties such as the preparation of test questions, grading, interview tests, document screening, and other matters necessary for the administration of tests:
  1. A person who has expert knowledge or competence in the relevant test field;
  2. A person who has extensive practical experience in duties to be performed upon appointment.
  (2) Allowances shall be paid to examiners within the budget as determined by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency. <Amended on Sep. 9, 1999; May 24, 2004; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 36 (Measures against persons committing misconduct) (1) If a firefighting official commits misconduct in a test, the relevant test shall be suspended or nullified, and he or she shall not be permitted to take any test under this Decree for 5 years.
  (2) The authority administering the test shall notify the appointing authority of the list of persons who have committed misconduct under paragraph (1), and the appointing authority, upon receiving such notification, shall request a disciplinary resolution from the competent disciplinary committee.

Article 37 (Preparation of test promotion candidate list) (1) The appointing authority or the appointment-recommending authority shall prepare a promotion candidate list for promotion by test by rank for persons who pass the test under Article 34(4). <Amended on Mar. 31, 2005>
  (2) Promotion by test shall be made in accordance with the order of ranking on the test promotion candidate list prepared under paragraph (1).
  (3) If a person entered on the test promotion candidate list receives a disciplinary action of reduction of salary or more severe disciplinary action before being promoted, the appointing authority or the appointment-recommending authority shall remove such person from the test promotion candidate list. <Added on Dec. 27, 2010>

CHAPTER VI SPECIAL PROMOTION

Article 38 (Special promotion of persons of distinguished service) (1) Persons eligible for special promotion under Article 17 of the Act shall be firefighting officials falling under any of the following: <Amended on Apr. 16, 1991; Dec. 31, 1991; Jan. 20, 2003; Mar. 31, 2005; Jan. 6, 2015; Nov. 5, 2019; Mar. 10, 2020; Jul. 7, 2025>
  1. Where a person falls under Article 40-4(1)1 of the State Public Officials Act: A person who is recognized as having rendered meritorious service and who, with integrity and a spirit of service, has devoted himself or herself to his or her duties and serves as a model for other public officials;
  2. A person who falls under Article 40-4(1)2 of the State Public Officials Act: Any of the following persons:
  a. A person who has outstanding ability to perform duties and is recognized by the appointing authority as having made significant contributions to the development of firefighting administration;
  b. A person who has received an award under Article 35-2(1)2b of the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials;
  2-2. Deleted; <Mar. 10, 2020>
  3. Where a person falls under Article 40-4(1)3 of the State Public Officials Act: A person who has received a bronze prize or higher in a proposal award and has made clearly distinguished contributions to the development of firefighting administration;
  4. Where a person falls under Article 40-4(1)4 of the State Public Officials Act: A person who has served for at least 20 years and voluntarily retires at least 1 year before reaching the retirement age, and who is recognized as having rendered meritorious service during his or her tenure;
  5. In the case of death in the line of duty: A person who is a public official who died in the line of duty under Article 3(1)3 of the Public Officials' Accident Compensation Act or a public official who died in the line of duty while performing hazardous duties under Article 3(1)4 of that Act, and who is recognized by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency or the Mayor/Do Governor as having rendered meritorious service during his or her tenure;
  6. Deleted. <Mar. 10, 2020>
  (2) Persons eligible for special promotion under the proviso of Article 17 of the Act shall be public officials who died in the line of duty under Article 3(1)3 of the Public Officials' Accident Compensation Act or who died in the line of duty while performing hazardous duties under Article 3(1)4 of that Act, who, by risking their lives and making devoted efforts, has performed remarkably distinguished service and died or died from injuries so sustained, or who, in the course of performing duties, has performed exemplary service serving as a model for others and died or died from injuries so sustained, and who are recognized by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency or the Mayor/Do Governor as having rendered meritorious service during his or her tenure. <Amended on Jan. 6, 2015; Mar. 10, 2020; Jul. 7, 2025>
  (3) The meritorious service rendered by a person of distinguished service under paragraph (1)1 through 3 shall be limited to the service rendered by the firefighting official at the relevant rank. <Amended on Jan. 6, 2015; Nov. 5, 2019; Mar. 10, 2020>
  (4) If a person falling under paragraph (1)2b has received a commendation at least equivalent to a Prime Minister's Commendation determined by the Minister of Personnel Management in accordance with Article 35-2(5) of the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials, he or she may be specially promoted beyond the authorized number of personnel for each rank, and the number corresponding to such person shall be deemed to be separately allocated to the relevant agency until the authorized number of personnel and the current number of personnel coincide. <Added on Nov. 5, 2019; Mar. 10, 2020>
  (5) Matters necessary for the procedures, operation, etc. of special promotion, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be determined by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency. <Added on Mar. 10, 2020>

Article 39 (Scope of ranks for special promotion) Special promotion under Article 38(1) shall be limited to promotion to the ranks according to the following classifications:
  1. If a person falls under Article 38(1)1 through 3: Promotion to a rank not higher than a deputy fire chief;
  2. If a person falls under Article 38(1)4: Promotion to a rank not higher than a deputy fire commissioner.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 10, 2020]

Article 40 (Implementation of special promotion) Special promotion of firefighting officials may be implemented whenever the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency or the Mayor/Do Governor deems it necessary. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 27, 2010]

Article 41 (Exclusion from application of the minimum length of service) (1) In cases of special promotion of a firefighting official who falls under Article 38(1)5 or paragraph (2) of that Article, Articles 4 through 6 shall not apply. <Amended on Aug. 31, 2021>
  (2) Special promotion under Article 38(1)1 and 3 shall be implemented from among persons whose period of service in the relevant rank is at least 2/3 of the minimum length of service required for promotion under Article 5(1) and who are not subject to any restriction on promotion appointment under Article 6. <Amended on Jan. 2, 2024>
  (3) The minimum length of service required for promotion under Article 5(1) shall not apply to special promotion under Article 38(1)2; provided, such special promotion shall be implemented from among persons who are not subject to any restriction on promotion appointment under Article 6. <Added on Jan. 2, 2024>
  (4) The minimum length of service required for promotion under Article 5(1) shall not apply to special promotion under Article 38(1)4; provided, such special promotion shall be implemented from among persons who are not subject to any restriction on promotion appointment under Article 6, except where a person falls under Article 6(1)4 through 6. <Added on Dec. 27, 2010; Dec. 21, 2011; Mar. 28, 2023; Jan. 2, 2024>

Article 41-2 (Restriction and revocation of special promotion) (1) If a special promotion appointment is made under Article 38(1)4, the relevant firefighting official shall have not have been subject to a severe disciplinary action during his or her period of service, nor to a minor disciplinary action on any of the following grounds: <Amended on Mar. 10, 2020>
  1. Grounds for disciplinary action under each subparagraph of Article 78-2(1) of the State Public Officials Act;
  2. Commission of a sexual crime defined in Article 2 of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes;
  3. Engagement in a commercial sex act defined in Article 2(1)1 of the Act on the Punishment of Arrangement of Commercial Sex Acts;
  4. Sexual harassment defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Gender Equality;
  5. Driving while intoxicated under Article 44(1) of the Road Traffic Act or refusal to submit to a breath test under Article 44(2) of that Act.
  (2) If a person specially promoted under Article 38(1)4 falls under Article 74-2(3)1, 1-2, or 1-3 of the State Public Officials Act and the honorary retirement allowance is to be recovered, the special promotion appointment shall be revoked. In such cases, the person whose special promotion appointment has been revoked shall be construed as having retired at the rank held prior to such special promotion appointment. <Amended on Mar. 10, 2020>
  (3) The Commissioner of the National Fire Agency or the Mayor/Do Governor may revoke a special promotion appointment if a person specially promoted under Article 38(1)5, paragraph (2) of that Article, and Article 42(1) through (3) falls under any of the following subparagraphs; provided, in cases falling under subparagraphs 1 and 3, he or she shall follow the results of review by the promotion review committee unless there is a compelling reason not to do so, and in cases falling under subparagraph 2, he or she shall revoke the relevant special promotion appointment: <Added on Jul. 7, 2025>
  1. Where, in examining whether to grant ex post facto approval of a special promotion appointment under the latter part of Article 42(3), the promotion review committee resolves that such special promotion appointment is inappropriate;
  2. Where, in the procedure for determining benefits under the Public Officials' Accident Compensation Act (referring to accident survivors' benefits under subparagraph 5b or c of Article 8 of that Act), the Minister of Personnel Management decides not to pay such benefits on the ground that the relevant person is not recognized as a public official who died in the line of duty under Article 3(1)3 of that Act or as a public official who died in the line of duty while performing hazardous duties under subparagraph 4 of that paragraph;
  3. Where it is clear, for reasons other than those specified in subparagraph 2, that a person does not fall under Article 38(1)5 or paragraph (2) of that Article; in such cases, the matter shall be subject to review by the promotion review committee.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 25, 2019]

Article 42 (Special promotion review) (1) Special promotion reviews of firefighting officials of the National Fire Agency and its affiliated institutions, the Fire Headquarters Chief, and the head of a local fire service academy shall be conducted by the Central Promotion Review Committee of the National Fire Agency. <Amended on Dec. 31, 1991; Sep. 9, 1999; May 24, 2004; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Mar. 10, 2020>
  (2) Special promotion reviews of firefighting officials for whom the Mayor/Do Governor exercises the appointing authority under Article 3(1) and 3(5)1 and 3 of the Decree on the Appointment of Firefighting Officials shall be conducted by the general promotion review committee established in the relevant City/Do. <Amended on Dec. 31, 1991; Sep. 9, 1999; Jan. 6, 2015; Mar. 10, 2020; Aug. 13, 2024>
  (3) Notwithstanding Article 38(1)5, 38(2), and Article 42(1) and (2), the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency or the Mayor/Do Governor may grant a special promotion appointment without undergoing review by the promotion review committee if a firefighting official dies from an injury sustained in the course of performing duties at the scene, the circumstances of death are clear, and he or she is recognized as having rendered distinguished service during his or her tenure. In such cases, the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency or the Mayor/Do Governor shall obtain ex post approval from the promotion review committee within the period under Article 35-2(9) of the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials. <Amended on Jul. 7, 2025>
  (4) If the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency or the Mayor/Do Governor grants a special promotion appointment under paragraph (3), he or she shall notify the survivors of the person so specially promoted that such special promotion appointment may be revoked if ex post facto approval is not obtained from the promotion review committee. <Added on Jul. 7, 2025>
  (5) Matters necessary for special promotion reviews shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Jul. 7, 2025>

CHAPTER VII PUBLIC OFFICIALS WITH EQUIVALENT RANK

Article 43 (Public officials with equivalent rank) (1) The appointing authority or the appointment-recommending authority may select, from among firefighting officials under his or her jurisdiction, a person who has served for at least the minimum length of service required for promotion in the relevant rank, who is not subject to any restriction on promotion appointment (excluding grounds under Article 6(1)4 through 6), and who has outstanding performance, as a public official as holding the immediately higher rank (hereinafter referred to as "public official with equivalent rank"). <Amended on May 6, 2015; Mar. 28, 2023>
  (2) Matters necessary for the selection of public officials with equivalent rank shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (3) Allowances may be paid to public officials with equivalent rank as prescribed by the Regulations on Allowances for Public Officials. <Amended on May 2, 2017; Mar. 10, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 24, 2008]

